// RUDOLF STEINER: THE SUBMERGED CONTINENTS OF ATLANTIS AND LEMURIA (LONDON, 1911),
// the first English of his articles "Aus der Akasha-Chronik" (Lucifer-Gnosis,
// 1904–08).
//
// The text comes from steiner-text.ts, generated by
// scripts/texts/steiner-atlantis.py from the Rudolf Steiner Archive's
// transcription of the 1911 book, compared word by word with an earlier state
// of the same transcription (an archive.org print-out of 2011, read through
// its OCR) and with the four phrases The Bookman quoted in September 1911.
// ONLY ONE TRANSCRIPTION OF THE 1911 ENGLISH COULD BE REACHED, and no page
// image of the book: the two states of the e-text are not independent copies,
// so no majority reading of independent witnesses could be formed. The script
// lists every difference, the two readings left disputed, and the two
// corrections made by hand (each checked against the German).
//
// The whole book is imported: ten chapters, divided by the archive into 26
// sections that follow the Archive's paragraph numbers, under a record for the
// book and one for each chapter. The German is public domain but cited, not
// transcribed: the only German reached is a later edition's, not the articles
// as first printed. Cosmic Memory (1959) and later translations are in
// copyright and cited only.
//
// THE AKASHIC RECORDS. Steiner presents the chapters as read in the "Akashic
// Records", a record of the past he says trained seers can perceive. The
// archive records that as his claim and gives it no year; it dates the
// writing to the Lucifer-Gnosis articles (1904–08) and the English to 1911.
// His root races and sub-races follow Theosophical writing, which he names:
// W. Scott-Elliot's The Story of Atlantis (1896), the "Secret Doctrine" of
// H. P. Blavatsky, and "Theosophical literature" in general. His links to
// Plato and to Blavatsky are recorded as what they are, later retellings or
// conceptual parallels; none claims ancient transmission. The book ranks
// human groups as advanced or "degraded" races; that racial theory is the
// text's own and is given as printed.
//
// Nothing is marked verified, and the sections are the archive's division,
// not a count stated by an edition (inventoryVerified stays false).

